      "text": "Here is a tailored breakdown based on your parameters. \n\nHaving **32GB of RAM** is fantastic because it means once you train the model on a free cloud GPU, you can easily run very capable models (up to 14B parameters, like Qwen2.5-14B or Llama-3.1-8B) entirely locally on your Intel laptop.\n\nRegarding **IPEX (Intel Extension for PyTorch)**: You are largely correct in your intuition. While Intel still technically updates it for their massive enterprise enterprise chips (like Gaudi or Max Series GPUs), **for consumer laptops, it is effectively a dead end.** The open-source LLM community focuses 99% of its effort on NVIDIA (CUDA). Getting IPEX to work with modern LLM fine-tuning libraries is a dependency nightmare, and even if it works, CPU training is painfully slow. \n\nSince you want to **Fine-Tune** and prefer **Free GPUs**, here are the best alternatives to get the job done. \n\n---\n\n### The Workflow: Cloud Training $\\rightarrow$ Local Intel Execution\nThe standard practice for your hardware situation is:\n1. Prepare your Git/Chat data locally.\n2. Upload it to a **Free GPU** (Google Colab or Kaggle).\n3. Fine-tune a LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) adapter.\n4. Export the fine-tuned model as a **GGUF file** while still in the cloud.\n5. Download the `.gguf` file to your Intel laptop and run it using your 32GB of RAM.\n\nHere are the best free GPU platforms and the frameworks you should use.\n\n---\n\n### Alternative 1: Unsloth on Google Colab (The Absolute Best)\n**Unsloth** is currently the gold standard for fine-tuning on free GPUs. It is a highly optimized library that rewrites the math behind training to make it run **2x faster** and use **70% less VRAM**.\n* **The Free GPU:** Google Colab (Free Tier) gives you an NVIDIA T4 GPU with 15GB of VRAM. Unsloth easily fits an 8B parameter model into this.\n* **Why it fits your use case:** Unsloth has built-in, 1-click cells to export your trained model directly into a `GGUF` file. \n* **How to do it:** \n  1. Go to the Unsloth GitHub page.\n  2. Open their standard \"ChatML\" or \"Alpaca\" Google Colab notebook.\n  3. Upload your JSONL file of Git history and chats.\n  4. Run the cells. It takes about 1-3 hours.\n  5. Run the final cell to save to GGUF and download it.\n\n### Alternative 2: LLaMA-Factory on Kaggle (The No-Code / UI Option)\nIf you don\u0027t want to mess with Python code and prefer a Web User Interface, **LLaMA-Factory** is an incredible tool. \n* **The Free GPU:** **Kaggle** (owned by Google) is heavily recommended here. Kaggle gives you **30 hours a week of free dual NVIDIA T4 GPUs**, which is much more generous than Google Colab\u0027s strict timeout limits.\n* **Why it fits your use case:** You can run LLaMA-Factory directly in a Kaggle notebook and it will generate a web link (via Gradio). You click the link, and you get a beautiful UI where you just select your base model, select your uploaded dataset, and click \"Start Training.\"\n* **How to do it:** Look up \"LLaMA-Factory Kaggle Notebook\". You upload your dataset, map the columns (Instruction/Response), and let it train.\n\n### Alternative 3: Axolotl (The Professional/Declarative Option)\nIf you are a developer who wants highly reproducible results and wants to understand how top-tier open-source models are made, use **Axolotl**.\n* **The Free GPU:** Kaggle or Google Colab, but since Axolotl is slightly heavier, Kaggle is preferred.\n* **Why it fits your use case:** Instead of writing training loops, you define everything in a single `YAML` configuration file. You specify the model, the dataset path, the learning rate, and the prompt format in one file, and run `accelerate launch -m axolotl.cli.train your_config.yaml`.\n* **Note:** It has a steeper learning curve than Unsloth, but it supports the widest variety of complex datasets (e.g., mixing chat history format with raw code diff formats).\n\n### Alternative 4: Hugging Face AutoTrain (The Easiest CLI)\nIf you just want to run a single command in a terminal to get your model, Hugging Face provides AutoTrain Advanced.\n* **The Free GPU:** Google Colab.\n* **Why it fits your use case:** You literally just install it and pass your data through a command-line argument.\n* **How to do it:** Open Colab, install the library, and run:\n  ```bash\n  autotrain llm --train --project-name my-git-model --model meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B --data-path ./my_data --peft --quantization int4 --lr 2e-4\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### Summary Recommendation for Your Specific Setup\n1. **Prepare your Data:** Combine your git commits and chat logs into a `JSONL` file formatted in ChatML (User/Assistant).\n2. **Train:** Open a Free **Google Colab** and use the **Unsloth** notebook. It is objectively the most painless way to squeeze fine-tuning onto a free tier GPU.\n3. **Export:** Use Unsloth\u0027s built-in export cell to save the model as a `Q4_K_M.gguf` file.\n4. **Run Locally:** Download that file to your Intel laptop. Since you have **32GB of RAM**, install **LM Studio** or **Ollama**, load the `.gguf` file, and you will get fast, responsive generation powered entirely by your system RAM and CPU.",
