        "text": "-14B or Llama-3.1-8B) entirely locally on your Intel laptop.\n\nRegarding"
      }, {
        "text": " **IPEX (Intel Extension for PyTorch)**: You are largely correct in your intuition. While Intel still technically updates it for"
      }, {
        "text": " their massive enterprise enterprise chips (like Gaudi or Max Series GPUs), **for consumer laptops, it is effectively a dead end.**"
      }, {
        "text": " The open-source LLM community focuses 99% of its effort on NVIDIA (CUDA). Getting IPEX to work with"
      }, {
        "text": " modern LLM fine-tuning libraries is a dependency nightmare, and even if it works, CPU training is painfully slow. \n\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Since you want to **Fine-Tune** and prefer **Free GPUs**, here are the best alternatives to get the job"
      }, {
        "text": " done. \n\n---\n\n### The Workflow: Cloud Training $\\rightarrow$ Local Intel Execution\nThe standard practice for your"
      }, {
        "text": " hardware situation is:\n1. Prepare your Git/Chat data locally.\n2. Upload it to a **Free GPU** ("
      }, {
        "text": "Google Colab or Kaggle).\n3. Fine-tune a LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) adapter.\n4."
      }, {
        "text": " Export the fine-tuned model as a **GGUF file** while still in the cloud.\n5. Download the"
      }, {
        "text": " `.gguf` file to your Intel laptop and run it using your 32GB of RAM.\n\nHere are the best"
      }, {
        "text": " free GPU platforms and the frameworks you should use.\n\n---\n\n### Alternative 1: Unsloth on Google Colab ("
      }, {
        "text": "The Absolute Best)\n**Unsloth** is currently the gold standard for fine-tuning on free GPUs. It is a"
      }, {
        "text": " highly optimized library that rewrites the math behind training to make it run **2x faster** and use **70% less"
      }, {
        "text": " VRAM**.\n* **The Free GPU:** Google Colab (Free Tier) gives you an NVIDIA T4 GPU"
      }, {
        "text": " with 15GB of VRAM. Unsloth easily fits an 8B parameter model into this.\n*"
      }, {
        "text": " **Why it fits your use case:** Unsloth has built-in, 1-click cells to export your trained model directly"
      }, {
        "text": " into a `GGUF` file. \n* **How to do it:** \n  1. Go"
      }, {
        "text": " to the Unsloth GitHub page.\n  2. Open their standard \"ChatML\" or \"Alpaca\" Google"
      }, {
        "text": " Colab notebook.\n  3. Upload your JSONL file of Git history and chats.\n  4. Run the"
      }, {
        "text": " cells. It takes about 1-3 hours.\n  5. Run the final cell to save to GGUF and"
      }, {
        "text": " download it.\n\n### Alternative 2: LLaMA-Factory on Kaggle (The No-Code / UI"
      }, {
        "text": " Option)\nIf you don\u0027t want to mess with Python code and prefer a Web User Interface, **LLa"
      }, {
        "text": "MA-Factory** is an incredible tool. \n* **The Free GPU:** **Kaggle** (owned"
      }, {
        "text": " by Google) is heavily recommended here. Kaggle gives you **30 hours a week of free dual NVIDIA T4 GPUs**,"
      }, {
        "text": " which is much more generous than Google Colab\u0027s strict timeout limits.\n* **Why it fits your use case:**"
      }, {
        "text": " You can run LLaMA-Factory directly in a Kaggle notebook and it will generate a web link (via Gradio). You"
      }, {
        "text": " click the link, and you get a beautiful UI where you just select your base model, select your uploaded dataset, and click"
      }, {
        "text": " \"Start Training.\"\n* **How to do it:** Look up \"LLaMA-Factory Kaggle Notebook\"."
      }, {
        "text": " You upload your dataset, map the columns (Instruction/Response), and let it train.\n\n### Alternative 3: Axolotl"
      }, {
        "text": " (The Professional/Declarative Option)\nIf you are a developer who wants highly reproducible results and wants to understand how"
      }, {
        "text": " top-tier open-source models are made, use **Axolotl**.\n* **The Free GPU:** Kag"
      }, {
        "text": "gle or Google Colab, but since Axolotl is slightly heavier, Kaggle is preferred.\n* **Why"
      }, {
        "text": " it fits your use case:** Instead of writing training loops, you define everything in a single `YAML` configuration file. You specify"
      }, {
        "text": " the model, the dataset path, the learning rate, and the prompt format in one file, and run `accelerate launch"
      }, {
        "text": " -m axolotl.cli.train your_config.yaml`.\n* **Note:** It has a steeper learning curve"
      }, {
        "text": " than Unsloth, but it supports the widest variety of complex datasets (e.g., mixing chat history format with raw"
      }, {
        "text": " code diff formats).\n\n### Alternative 4: Hugging Face AutoTrain (The Easiest CLI)\nIf you just"
      }, {
        "text": " want to run a single command in a terminal to get your model, Hugging Face provides AutoTrain Advanced.\n* **"
      }, {
        "text": "The Free GPU:** Google Colab.\n* **Why it fits your use case:** You literally just install it and pass"
      }, {
        "text": " your data through a command-line argument.\n* **How to do it:** Open Colab, install the library, and run"
      }, {
        "text": ":\n  ```bash\n  autotrain llm --train --project-name my-git-model --model meta"
      }, {
        "text": "-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B --data-path ./my_data --peft --quant"
      }, {
        "text": "ization int4 --lr 2e-4\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### Summary Recommendation for Your Specific Setup\n"
      }, {
        "text": "1. **Prepare your Data:** Combine your git commits and chat logs into a `JSONL` file formatted in Chat"
      }, {
        "text": "ML (User/Assistant).\n2. **Train:** Open a Free **Google Colab** and use the **Un"
      }, {
        "text": "sloth** notebook. It is objectively the most painless way to squeeze fine-tuning onto a free tier GPU.\n3."
      }, {
        "text": " **Export:** Use Unsloth\u0027s built-in export cell to save the model as a `Q4_"
      }, {
        "text": "K_M.gguf` file.\n4. **Run Locally:** Download that file to your Intel laptop. Since"
      }, {
        "text": " you have **32GB of RAM**, install **LM Studio** or **Ollama**, load the `.gguf"
      }, {
        "text": "` file, and you will get fast, responsive generation powered entirely by your system RAM and CPU."
